[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JSX: Syntax, Expressions, and Compilation in React"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"JSX (JavaScript XML) is a syntax extension to JavaScript. It allows you to write HTML-like structures within your JavaScript code. These structures are then transformed into regular JavaScript function calls. This tutorial will cover JSX syntax, how to embed expressions, and the compilation process."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"JSX Syntax"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"JSX looks very similar to HTML, but there are some key differences to keep in mind."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Structure"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"A JSX expression must have one and only one outermost element. This is often referred to as the 'root' element. If you need to render multiple elements, you must wrap them in a single parent element like a `<div>`, `<>` (Fragment), or another custom component."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"<div>
  <h1>Hello, world!</h1>
  <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
</div>"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following example is invalid because it has two root elements:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"<h1>Hello, world!</h1>
<p>This is a paragraph.</p>  // Invalid JSX"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To fix this, wrap the elements in a single parent element:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"<>
  <h1>Hello, world!</h1>
  <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
</>"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"HTML Attributes vs. JSX Attributes"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Some HTML attributes have different names in JSX. For example, `class` becomes `className`, and `for` becomes `htmlFor`. This is because `class` and `for` are reserved keywords in JavaScript."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"<div className="container">
  <label htmlFor="name">Name:</label>
  <input type="text" id="name" />
</div>"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Self-Closing Tags"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"HTML allows some tags to be self-closing (e.g., `<br>`, `<input>`). In JSX, you must explicitly close these tags with a forward slash: `<br />`, `<input />`."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"<img src="image.jpg" alt="My Image" />
<input type="text" />"},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"Embedding Expressions in JSX"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can embed JavaScript expressions within JSX using curly braces `{}`. This allows you to dynamically render content, perform calculations, and pass data to components."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Rendering Variables"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"const name = 'John Doe';

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>
  );
}"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Using JavaScript Expressions"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"function formatName(user) {
  return user.firstName + ' ' + user.lastName;
}

const user = {
  firstName: 'Jane',
  lastName: 'Doe'
};

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <h1>Hello, {formatName(user)}!</h1>
  );
}"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Conditional Rendering"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use JavaScript's conditional operators (ternary operator, `&&`) to conditionally render elements in JSX."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"function MyComponent(props) {
  const isLoggedIn = props.isLoggedIn;
  return (
    <div>
      {isLoggedIn ? (
        <h1>Welcome back!</h1>
      ) : (
        <h1>Please sign up.</h1>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Or, using the `&&` operator:"},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"function MyComponent(props) {
  const isLoggedIn = props.isLoggedIn;
  return (
    <div>
      {isLoggedIn && <h1>Welcome back!</h1>}
      {!isLoggedIn && <h1>Please sign up.</h1>}
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Rendering Lists"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use the `map()` function to iterate over an array and render a list of elements.  Remember to include a unique `key` prop for each item in the list. The `key` prop helps React identify which items have changed, are added, or are removed."},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

function NumberList(props) {
  const numbers = props.numbers;
  const listItems = numbers.map((number) =>
    <li key={number.toString()}>
      {number}
    </li>
  );
  return (
    <ul>{listItems}</ul>
  );
}"},{"order":32,"type":"heading","text":"JSX Compilation"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"JSX is not directly understood by browsers. It needs to be transformed into regular JavaScript code that browsers can execute. This transformation is typically done using a tool called Babel."},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"Babel"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"Babel is a JavaScript compiler that can transform JSX into standard JavaScript. When you use a tool like Create React App, Babel is already configured for you.  However, it's helpful to understand what's happening under the hood."},{"order":36,"type":"subheading","text":"Transformation Process"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"Babel transforms JSX elements into `React.createElement()` calls.  `React.createElement()` creates JavaScript objects that describe the elements you want to render.  React then uses these objects to build the actual DOM."},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, the following JSX:"},{"order":39,"type":"code","text":"<h1>Hello, world!</h1>"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"is transformed into:"},{"order":41,"type":"code","text":"React.createElement(
  "h1",
  null,
  "Hello, world!"
)"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"And this JSX:"},{"order":43,"type":"code","text":"<div className="container">
  <p>Hello</p>
</div>"},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"is transformed into:"},{"order":45,"type":"code","text":"React.createElement(
  "div",
  {
    className: "container"
  },
  React.createElement(
    "p",
    null,
    "Hello"
  )
)"},{"order":46,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `React.createElement` function takes three arguments:"},{"order":47,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The tag name (e.g., 'div', 'h1', 'p')."},{"order":48,"type":"bullet_point","text":"An object containing the attributes (props) for the element."},{"order":49,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The children of the element (which can be text, other elements, or null)."},{"order":50,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":51,"type":"paragraph","text":"JSX provides a concise and readable way to define UI components in React. By understanding its syntax, how to embed expressions, and the compilation process, you can effectively build dynamic and interactive user interfaces."}]
